Found by lane-resolver, 2026-08-31, answering a question I asked after the
SECOND instance in one week. It is a pattern, not three bugs.

## The measured table

| declared modifier | parser records | reaches the pool |
|---|---|---|
| `is_const` (method) | yes | **now** — B101 |
| `is_mixin` (class) | yes | **now** — B111 |
| `is_shared` (function, class, interface, enum, funcdef) | yes | **NO** |
| `is_abstract` (class) | yes | **NO** |
| `is_override` / `is_final` | yes | **NO** |
| `is_private` / `is_protected` | yes | **NO** |

`shared` is never pooled at all — the only occurrences under `symbols/` are
test strings.

## THE GENERALISATION, which is the reason this is one ticket

`SymbolKind` records what a symbol **is**, not what it was **declared as**. So
every declaration modifier has to be re-derived from the AST by whoever wants
it — and that only works **within one file**, because an AST sidecar is built
per document while the pool is the **cross-file** surface.

That is the actual defect. It predicts exactly where the next instance bites:
any rule needing a modifier for a symbol declared in ANOTHER file.

## Three sidecars already exist to work around it

* `shared_declaration_roots` (`analysis/unused.rs:459`) — B087 needed `shared`
  and walked the AST per file to get it.
* `file_const_methods` (`typecheck/checker.rs`) — reported during B101. Its sole
  `NonConstMethodOnConstReceiver` emit site sits inside the `file_classes` arm,
  so **a const method on a class declared in another file has no const metadata
  at all.** That is a live cross-file gap, not a hypothetical one.
* A third for the mixin/abstract family.

Retiring these is the deliverable, not just adding fields. A sidecar that stays
after the pool can answer is a second source of truth that will drift.

## Cost of NOT doing it, concretely

`is_private` is the one waiting. Completion has **no visibility filtering at
all** today — no `private` handling anywhere in `completion.rs` — and it cannot
be written without pooled visibility. So this ticket is a prerequisite for a
user-visible feature, not only cleanup.

## The two instances already fixed both cost a real defect

Neither was noticed as "a field is missing". Each was found by the FALSE OUTPUT
it caused, one ticket at a time:

* B101: `void M() const` and `void M()` were indistinguishable to every
  consumer, so B094 shipped with a deliberate hole — and B095's code action
  silently stopped being offered for const methods, because it parses a rendered
  signature and `strip_suffix(')')` returns `None` on a trailing `const`.
* B111: a `mixin class` was flagged for not implementing an interface it never
  owed, a false ERROR on two SHIPPING plugins.

At two instances that is coincidence. At three-plus with the mechanism named, a
systematic fix is cheaper than waiting for each to surface as a user-visible
defect.

## Scope discipline

`is_abstract` is provably harmless RIGHT NOW — B094 measured that `abstract`
does not exempt, and `an_abstract_class_is_not_exempt` pins it. So do not
justify this ticket on `is_abstract`; it is carried for uniformity, and say so.

Add the fields, retire the sidecars, and — per B101's own hazards entry — do
NOT treat the compile errors as the affected set. Enumerating them tells you
where a TYPE changed, not where BEHAVIOUR did. B101's one real breakage was
invisible to the compiler because the consumer parsed a rendered string rather
than reading the enum. Look for consumers that re-derive a modifier from text or
from an AST walk, not only those that destructure the enum.

## Related

B101, B111 (the two fixed instances), B087 (`shared_declaration_roots`), B094
(the `abstract` measurement), B095 (the invisible breakage), and the hazards
entry "'Add the field and see what breaks' cannot see a consumer that parses the
rendered string".

## Lane decision (2026-08-31, bl-fix-many batch B113-114)
Serialized AFTER B113 in one worktree (.worktrees/pool) — B113 edits hover.rs, which is inside this ticket's consumer blast radius (SymbolKind patterns in 16 files). Implement only once B113 is committed in the lane.
## Decision (coordinator): scope confirmed as add-fields + retire-all-three-sidecars, per the ticket's own deliverable statement. is_abstract carried for uniformity only (B094's pin stays authoritative). Completion visibility filtering (is_private consumer) is explicitly OUT of scope — this ticket makes it possible, a follow-up ships it.

Lane decisions recorded by coordinator (implementation complete on lane/pool, review in flight):

1. PLACEMENT: DeclModifiers lives on Symbol, not per-variant in SymbolKind. Both designs were derived independently (this lane planned per-variant; the peer's stood-down resolver lane built Symbol-level in lane/resolver-b114-wip). Deciding argument: shared_declaration_roots asks one question of five kinds — at Symbol level its retirement is one expression; per-variant needs a five-arm match at the call site, recreating the 'same question through N shapes' defect this ticket targets. The per-variant objection (a Variable answering is_private:false when the parser dropped field visibility) survives the change of shape but not of layer: per-variant would only make the wrong answer unaskable, not right. Peer's WIP branch did not compile against current master (cut before B081 added a SymbolKind::Class construction site); fixed and re-measured rather than trusting inherited green.

2. SCOPE: retire TWO sidecars, not three. ClassCtx.is_mixin stays — it reads the node the walker is descending into (lexical context), no name lookup, no cross-file question. Reached independently by this lane and by the ticket author's resolver lane. Decisive test: no case can be written that fails before and passes after, which by this repo's standard makes retiring it a refactor with a false story.

3. get_/set_ aliasing trap (two independent derivations, confirmed): file_const_methods aliased the STRIPPED property name; the pool does not. workspace_method_is_const tries C::X then falls back through accessor spellings.

4. Field visibility stays OUT (parser drops it at ClassMember::Field — no is_private on VarDecl). Pinned with a named RECORDED-NOT-FIXED test asserting the known-wrong value plus positive control. Peer is filing the parser-level fix as a separate ticket.

Gate at checkpoint: SUITE-VERIFIED 2848/0/12, 33/33 binaries (= master 2807 + B113 21 + B114 20).

CORRECTION to the previous append, item 4: B115 merged (1614fc2) while B114 review was in flight — the parser NOW carries is_private/is_protected on VarDeclStmt and PropertyDecl, so 'field visibility stays out' is invalidated at its premise. Field visibility is now IN scope for B114: the field arm must pool it (it was hardcoding DeclModifiers::default(), a real gap post-B115, caught pre-merge by the ticket author against the live worktree), properties likewise, the RECORDED-NOT-FIXED pin inverts to assert the true values, and the stale scope.rs doc comment (claims parser drops it) must be updated. The pin as written was a self-referential probe — it asserted on the pooled value its own arm hardcoded, so the escape hatch in its message could never fire. Downstream: peer's B117 (completion visibility filtering) is blocked on these pooled flags; dot-member completion reads exclusively from the pool (completion.rs:340). Lane redirected before merge; new base 2823/0/12 34/34.